# @Number annotation on a String value — passes silently.
#
# Number is a typeclass (class [let n]) declared in prelude.llt. When the type checker
# processes @Number, resolve_type_head finds TypeStageEntry::Class and produces a fresh
# TypeVar with a Number class constraint. The lowerer emits TypeAssertCheck::Resolved(tv)
# where tv = TypeValue.Var(_t).
#
# At runtime, value_matches_type("not a number", TypeValue.Var(_t)) delegates to BAS,
# which returns true for TV_VAR on either side (conservative: unbound TypeVar is consistent
# with any value). The assertion therefore passes silently and the String is returned as-is.
#
# Contrast with @Integer: Integer resolves to TypeValue.Repr("Value::Int") (via Integer: TypeNode.Int
# in builtin_core.llt), which is a concrete repr type that fails for String values.
[@Number "not a number"]
=== out
String("not a number")
